      "title": "Soil Conditioning",
      "content": {
        "markup": "<p>In a soil laboratory, establishing the optimal conditions for testing is essential.</p>\n<p>Like all testing, sample conditioning follows step-by-step methodologies to ensure consistency of results.</p>\n<p>Soil conditioning or preparation can take up to 24 hours.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>How are samples conditioned?</strong><br />Samples may be riffled, dried, sieved, soaked, heated, hydrated or compacted for a range of tests.</p>\n<p>Soil samples are prepared using various techniques as required for testing such as Maximum Dry Density (MDD), Optimum Moisture Content (OMC), California Bearing Ratio (CBR) , Unconfined Compressive Strength (UCS) or Indirect Tensile Strength (ITS).</p>",
        "blocks": {
          "contentData": [],
          "settingsData": []
        }
      },
      "images": [
        {
          "key": "417ce6fc-30ce-4fbb-ba7a-5fe5641c7d55",
          "mediaKey": "59db0f57-4c18-4b2c-bba1-fc86d69fe4dd"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "contentTypeKey": "3ef5c851-77d2-46af-9a22-e2bfadd01829",
      "udi": "umb://element/d47132f096fe4bb79c88e02a013406fa",
      "title": "Plasticity Index (PI)",
      "content": {
        "markup": "<p>The PI section of a soil laboratory assesses physical and chemical properties.</p>\n<p>This includes more delicate testing to determine moisture, plasticity, material classification and more.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Why perform PI testing?<br /></strong>Known as Atterberg Limits, these are key indicators of soil behavior under different moisture conditions.</p>\n<p>By determining the shrinkage limit, liquid limit, plastic limit and plasticity index, professionals gain insights into how the soil will behave under varying moisture conditions.</p>\n<p>Understanding moisture characteristics is crucial for compaction control, slope stability analysis and overall project planning, and is very commonly used in road construction.</p>",
        "blocks": {
          "contentData": [],
          "settingsData": []
        }
      },
      "images": [
        {
          "key": "9a989a8c-d0dd-494f-9377-272b2c733427",
          "mediaKey": "3962733b-b270-4f98-8c63-4932b5ddc46a"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "contentTypeKey": "3ef5c851-77d2-46af-9a22-e2bfadd01829",
      "udi": "umb://element/de9c061726c44dfda064ddc52f71303a",
      "title": "Density and Load Testing",
      "content": {
        "markup": "<p>These are the most commonly understood tests among construction professionals.</p>\n<p>These tests provide a comprehensive picture of the soil’s engineering properties, influencing critical decisions in foundation design, road works and major construction.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>What does this include?</strong><br />Maximum Dry Density (MDD) refers to the highest achievable density of a soil under specific compaction conditions, the point at which a soil achieves optimal compaction and, therefore, maximum stability.</p>\n<p>Optimum Moisture Content (OMC) represents the moisture level at which a soil attains its maximum density during compaction, a vital parameter indicating the precise moisture required for optimal compaction.</p>\n<p>California Bearing Ratio (CBR) is a key performance indicator for assessing the load-bearing capacity of a soil, crucial in pavement design and to help engineers evaluate the soil’s ability to support heavy loads.</p>\n<p>Unconfined Compressive Strength (UCS) provides insights into the soil’s structural integrity, particularly important in geotechnical engineering and to assess the strength of foundations and retaining walls.</p>\n<p>Indirect Tensile Strength (ITS) is a measure of a soil’s ability to resist tensile forces under indirect loading conditions, providing insights into the soil’s durability and behavior under different stress conditions.</p>",
        "blocks": {
          "contentData": [],
          "settingsData": []
        }
      },
      "images": [
        {
          "key": "1a0cedce-00c7-4fd8-a58d-db89799eff5c",
          "mediaKey": "9ee868ec-c1f3-423a-a3af-2196a567b032"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "contentTypeKey": "3b803f00-a335-4bc4-bb3c-49b71dedbcca",
